#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161805 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "6ae71f41-133f-50b6-a286-20ae7e2c3a9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8291" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\nII. $A$ is diagonalizable\n\nIII. If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are two eigenvectors, then $\\mathbf{u} \\perp \\mathbf{v}$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable. Hence, it is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors corresponding to the *same* eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161806 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/id #uuid "d185ef42-c177-5217-99b7-b42d800f3385" :question/math-academy-id "e-8292" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ of the matrix ![](images/b8/b83651102a8a5c6d19850a669c9805643af386eaec9a37439d5f9e2724a18134.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1\\cdot \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2\\cdot \\mathbf u_2^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, $\\mathbf u_i\\cdot \\mathbf u_i^T$ is the projection matrix corresponding to $\\mathbf u_i.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac12x_2.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/00/0095b4111c1b734f68f72a0f971d1e04e7ef568d39dd9bc512f63ab503edae9c.svg)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/bd/bd41201270ab718d62cdd853ca5f80b748e2a0f8a9c57f08ccbd4ab64fe51206.svg)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n![](images/fa/fa0933569543fe5857a2b058b278fdc52bb4d63f1569bb91b24a3ad8f6547745.svg)\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161807 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "65b23c21-528f-5df7-a00c-8e2dce041276" :question/math-academy-id "e-8293" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/f3/f3ca9b44bdc54fe81c68afe3fd9d10572cc65662db7380436020f8e120029785.svg) and ![](images/eb/eb5d75236a3dc64f0a45a24b6c2d7bde167977fec794ed6973dd867871119820.svg)\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-7,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1\\cdot \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2\\cdot \\mathbf u_2^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-7)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]+(-7)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-39 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "335bec9f-ed9d-56c8-916d-efad8de10be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477832} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca15352-c656-5341-bae1-c8a470935247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477959} {:answer/id #uuid "7108485d-b98a-52fa-b46f-6d7a305db6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477973} {:answer/id #uuid "d82ccaa3-d4c5-5973-8cb3-52eb54e5a251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478159} {:answer/id #uuid "dec57307-22b7-5f71-bc4e-cc3270d38fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d82ccaa3-d4c5-5973-8cb3-52eb54e5a251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478159} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5278afb-2096-505f-846f-dae67d414271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78ca88fc-6dc6-5bfd-8b4b-e3a86c40d462" :question/math-academy-id "q-140845" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all distinct eigenvalues\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u$ is parallel to $v.$\n\nIII. $A$ is diagonalizable." :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily distinct. This occurs when the characteristic polynomial has roots whose (algebraic) multiplicity is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. Since the eigenvectors are nonzero, we can't have $u$ parallel to $v.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebc86ce8-16cb-5d42-b93d-c901cdaee24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375960} {:answer/id #uuid "ca85089e-e474-5e06-acdb-067811b60e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375961} {:answer/id #uuid "c7fc5e9a-6bbb-5acf-b593-e2a428796fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375962} {:answer/id #uuid "7fcb31c1-0bbe-5c45-89d9-f858f8b6fa7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375963} {:answer/id #uuid "62d7ad13-ccac-52eb-b575-d07fab41300b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7fc5e9a-6bbb-5acf-b593-e2a428796fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375962} :answer-field/id #uuid "133635ad-f318-5cc6-977b-545f7d02dac9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e634704-5162-5c6f-bb3c-cfc5bc2ccc48" :question/math-academy-id "q-140846" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e9827-741e-554d-b4bd-a580c12832ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} {:answer/id #uuid "637ff16c-2a36-5623-9258-1f3130b80075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407529} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8f4850-6613-5ef7-a355-f12825aba902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407532} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7dec3-53c9-52fb-9b61-30210ef0b89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407538} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ed5d0-4d06-561d-902d-1213f1013134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "237e9827-741e-554d-b4bd-a580c12832ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7eee37-4841-5d7e-83f1-ad47a007de82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69f0b9bd-9336-5c1c-91e3-bf72b33b1333" :question/math-academy-id "q-140847" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259bc7f0-def1-5dbe-84fa-df884b75de5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365061} {:answer/id #uuid "4ede6774-72a7-5e72-98ce-278b61c0729e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365062} {:answer/id #uuid "a6077b11-1139-5964-95d7-ab7c61b27c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365063} {:answer/id #uuid "55529e55-64a4-5791-b0a3-817ff749c29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365064} {:answer/id #uuid "941d3dcd-c846-51d5-8b37-ac5967b27e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "259bc7f0-def1-5dbe-84fa-df884b75de5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365061} :answer-field/id #uuid "1feaaa2b-9f91-5604-bad3-f9a41903fec2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3897ca8e-c190-5166-8001-b5cc763dcea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140848" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "846e4813-0a6d-57cd-a2b0-218ce2770f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161163} {:answer/id #uuid "97aef119-82d0-5aa7-aa71-62e1a650df01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161164} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a800d7-d191-51d2-8c96-d5e7c1ce516b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161165} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8c8d85-d02d-5068-9a07-b1d987ef1a8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161166} {:answer/id #uuid "648cf5fc-f995-57c4-a3ec-98422a3fac9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97aef119-82d0-5aa7-aa71-62e1a650df01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161164} :answer-field/id #uuid "6154b143-fbfd-5485-b59a-fdf765587d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15b6a411-2b41-5f8b-b306-30b41e9bfcc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94766" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have $5$ distinct eigenvalues\n\nII. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable\n\nIII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue, then $u⊥v$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily distinct. This occurs when the characteristic polynomial has roots whose (algebraic) multiplicity is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal, but two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue may not be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82cbba1a-f069-5a33-a2d1-b502c99b2b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452203} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6ac186-7f49-5faa-93e6-ca668251994b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82cbba1a-f069-5a33-a2d1-b502c99b2b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452203} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e0776b3-44ee-59b9-afc3-45d560491387" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452949} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d482a9a1-475e-5fcc-bafe-deb4762a9d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452205} {:answer/id #uuid "54115a6d-0ff0-5c69-9194-8cdd62d05b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54115a6d-0ff0-5c69-9194-8cdd62d05b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452206} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4206f3a-3ee1-5441-9ffb-b57c2618eba7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452950} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3455c3f3-d151-520c-b72e-9874313639d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452207} {:answer/id #uuid "69ca346c-3af4-5c9e-91d8-6637dc8c1dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ca346c-3af4-5c9e-91d8-6637dc8c1dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452208} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa13589e-7777-52d2-8d09-0d1f83bbcf12" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42059a97-8b39-59cb-bae6-eab5799ef586" :question/math-academy-id "q-95409" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is not diagonalizable\n- **II.** If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$\n- **III.** $A$ must have $4$ real (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, hence, is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, a $4\\times 4$ matrix has $4$ (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues and the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3c6113a-67b8-5c18-9cc8-a4549599a0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161247} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c8950b-6785-5e3a-9045-4b8004fd0f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161248} {:answer/id #uuid "20b2ebd8-2bc6-5deb-90e2-8af6876b83aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161249} {:answer/id #uuid "23c5c152-ee2d-5c72-9f1a-c6ea18b9f513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161250} {:answer/id #uuid "30af77ee-d1d2-52e1-8553-be2d0df56f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4c8950b-6785-5e3a-9045-4b8004fd0f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161248} :answer-field/id #uuid "c096a467-6ace-5250-9b7e-9c8eac824f30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbaacbd1-c58a-5f9b-94e2-0510581c4052" :question/math-academy-id "q-95411" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\nII. $A$ is diagonalizable\n\nIII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha $" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, hence, is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. Since the eigenvector are nonzero, we can't have $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37efe81-e4e1-5a83-93e0-08cabbfe53d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452215} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6cbf3a-43ca-51ee-8eb3-b50854600205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a6cbf3a-43ca-51ee-8eb3-b50854600205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452216} :answer-field/id #uuid "479f0a5a-bddd-55ed-aa58-35002f747d6d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452955} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c13ebd-2fcf-502b-bb98-1a35a8d3954c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452217} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9346d4-95e8-50e0-a1f6-50b3b049ce5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e9346d4-95e8-50e0-a1f6-50b3b049ce5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452218} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fd6f8e-d6c4-55b8-83df-719636ccff26" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452956} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01499ffa-8b4c-5e53-aadb-bcf349545ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452219} {:answer/id #uuid "a978bc16-54ab-589d-955b-d621b0ea98ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01499ffa-8b4c-5e53-aadb-bcf349545ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452219} :answer-field/id #uuid "a126a47e-711d-55e4-b163-1b04af3cc6c1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "531e02eb-ae49-5805-b616-b28b7ddc3a97" :question/math-academy-id "q-95702" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ real symmetric matrix whose roots of the characteristic polynomial have multiplicity equal to $1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue, then $u$ is parallel to $v$\n- **II.** $A$ must have $7$ real and distinct eigenvalues\n- **III.** $A$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. But two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue will always be parallel if the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real. Also, since the roots of the characteristic polynomial have (algebraic) multiplicity all equal to $1,$ the eigenvalues are all distinct.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, but not necessarily orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "794d4c57-637a-5193-940b-4ff7ac74a55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395040} {:answer/id #uuid "27d3309c-3a7e-5f1c-908b-6094dc352bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395041} {:answer/id #uuid "380b57b2-e791-5039-9d04-a9810497ecb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395042} {:answer/id #uuid "4f13293f-f4e6-5635-a0a7-6f82d5e89e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395043} {:answer/id #uuid "870b3347-21ff-559c-b1a9-0d01055f883c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "380b57b2-e791-5039-9d04-a9810497ecb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395042} :answer-field/id #uuid "a837d983-c570-594e-8c53-2aa0ddbba19d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e789a84c-c48c-5446-b3d4-f3e93d458a29" :question/math-academy-id "q-95704" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =5.$ Computing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aff604c-8612-5d59-a83a-b4bff7afa693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366672} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba28056-be96-5bb5-9d00-cdb75f7c223c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366673} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4e70e8-049c-5b59-890d-0c8c7f808c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366674} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a55167-1c4d-5728-844c-1cb9a9a9cdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366675} {:answer/id #uuid "01361167-700e-598b-a8fe-1795f3c6300d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2a55167-1c4d-5728-844c-1cb9a9a9cdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366675} :answer-field/id #uuid "114fa9c1-9d2d-5e26-8ec3-22bb6c29182d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04f2e807-374a-54f0-9c1c-c36bd677ab53" :question/math-academy-id "q-95705" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdaa4fcd-63b1-53cf-99ad-69e5aa1715b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366679} {:answer/id #uuid "5c370ad2-76f7-57aa-b664-444a66d7107e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366680} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6815e5-353a-5da8-b4b2-bc8d0190c1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366681} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c8c172-b621-54a1-955b-6fc7cc1632a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366682} {:answer/id #uuid "70681932-2c89-5740-a12f-c05801e037bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdaa4fcd-63b1-53cf-99ad-69e5aa1715b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366679} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa6eca96-0d33-5d05-a114-f5e9e81a3f7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f428032d-6477-55fd-911d-d21c6e60fb59" :question/math-academy-id "q-95707" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =8.$ Computing $A-8I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d327dec-d42a-581c-8707-926d0b6893a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407522} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ec327-8643-5dfe-8735-8c0774db03ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407526} {:answer/id #uuid "799755e8-7ecf-5b56-8460-da2805b858a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407533} {:answer/id #uuid "95054c16-a500-55cc-9c6d-5dc257b93966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407534} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b2cd88-0c1c-5067-96b8-f34a70eda38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4b2cd88-0c1c-5067-96b8-f34a70eda38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb85388f-97d4-5b7a-9384-bb45d3a9d42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d231abd-db3d-5d11-9253-820e7eb51742" :question/math-academy-id "q-95712" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68d6bb6a-6b5d-5930-ae9a-9b839dcdddb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395061} {:answer/id #uuid "0347e36c-6386-5361-936c-dfdf6232dced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -15 \\\\ -15 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395062} {:answer/id #uuid "66380990-ae13-5c02-b2dc-9fd55b82a145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 14 \\\\ 14 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395063} {:answer/id #uuid "5e73c68a-ab95-5792-acd8-5957407b5f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395064} {:answer/id #uuid "f3057db3-3c43-50e8-88bc-8548c42643cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e73c68a-ab95-5792-acd8-5957407b5f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395064} :answer-field/id #uuid "425b9a82-dba0-5520-8aa0-36f3f21fc4a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "180de339-1f27-54e7-82a8-44bcd599a7e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95718" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-12,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(13\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(13\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]+(-12)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-75 & -300 \\\\ -300 & 100\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bc49024-62c3-5852-bb10-f8ca661deb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395075} {:answer/id #uuid "189c99b8-b12d-5bce-8a73-8432708f26d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395076} {:answer/id #uuid "22327ee0-66ed-5f0b-ad74-16db425a88e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395077} {:answer/id #uuid "9c64ad08-76b8-5693-907f-147d2c3ef11a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395078} {:answer/id #uuid "4535b0b1-6fc1-52dd-b0ee-ea5c7d2feecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "189c99b8-b12d-5bce-8a73-8432708f26d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -23\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395076} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a48dfc1-7406-5a03-80fb-7e45d0584999" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37b1654d-7a67-5975-aaea-e4e904cbf870" :question/math-academy-id "q-95729" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-24,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-24)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-24)\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-24)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}25 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-24)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 25\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 130 \\\\ 130 & -598\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -23\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0767736c-ac62-5871-9691-074ff1560dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366692} {:answer/id #uuid "2df3e6bc-5619-5bf0-a3a8-2cd3d18c7186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366693} {:answer/id #uuid "8a06b9c7-c193-5ce6-bc61-6ab0f9e839e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366694} {:answer/id #uuid "0e98a004-8e88-526a-883d-a269cd2144df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366695} {:answer/id #uuid "204e9a6c-25fc-58be-897b-15640eb44427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a06b9c7-c193-5ce6-bc61-6ab0f9e839e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366694} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7728008-cd80-5ab5-b45d-4c9fa5d487ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "205194a3-9e3c-52b4-993f-f1572b1527e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95730" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 20 \\\\ 20 & -15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
